sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/aristotle-ha06-36> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "In a cross between horse and ass, gestation lasts as long as it would for the sire's own species, while in size, looks and vigour the foal tends to resemble the mother more than the sire.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Hybrid gestation follows the sire; the foal resembles the mother";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Aristotle, The History of Animals (Historia Animalium), Book VI, Part 23; translated by D'Arcy Wentworth Thompson, The Works of Aristotle Translated into English, ed. J. A. Smith and W. D. Ross, Oxford: Clarendon Press, vol. IV (1910). Scope: mule and hinny (horse × donkey crosses)"
}
